DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ee22ffsscckk is used to check the ext2/ext3/ext4 family of file systems.  For
       ext3 and ext4 file systems that use a journal, if the system  has  been
       shut  down  uncleanly without any errors, normally, after replaying the
       committed transactions  in the  journal,  the  file  system  should  be
       marked  as clean.   Hence, for file systems that use journaling, ee22ffsscckk
       will normally replay the journal and exit, unless its superblock  indi‐
       cates that further checking is required.

       _d_e_v_i_c_e  is a block device (e.g., _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_c_1) or file containing the file
       system.

       Note that in general it is not safe to run ee22ffsscckk on mounted file  sys‐
       tems.  The only exception is if the --nn option is specified, and --cc, --ll,
       or --LL options are _n_o_t specified.   However, even if it is  safe  to  do
       so,  the  results printed by ee22ffsscckk are not valid if the file system is
       mounted.   If ee22ffsscckk asks whether or not you should check a file system
       which  is mounted, the only correct answer is ``no''.  Only experts who
       really know what they are doing should consider answering this question
       in any other way.

       If  ee22ffsscckk  is run in interactive mode (meaning that none of --yy, --nn, or
       --pp are specified), the program will ask the user to  fix  each  problem
       found  in  the  file system.  A response of 'y' will fix the error; 'n'
       will leave the error unfixed; and 'a' will fix the problem and all sub‐
       sequent  problems;  pressing  Enter  will  proceed with the default re‐
       sponse, which is printed before the question mark.  Pressing  Control-C
       terminates e2fsck immediately.

