RREEPPOORRTTIINNGG BBUUGGSS
       Almost  any  piece of software will have bugs.  If you manage to find a
       file system which causes ee22ffsscckk to crash, or which ee22ffsscckk is unable  to
       repair, please report it to the author.

       Please  include  as  much  information  as possible in your bug report.
       Ideally, include a complete transcript of the ee22ffsscckk run, so I can  see
       exactly  what  error  messages  are displayed.  (Make sure the messages
       printed by ee22ffsscckk are in English; if your system has been configured so
       that  ee22ffsscckk's  messages  have  been  translated into another language,
       please set the the LLCC__AALLLL environment variable to CC so that  the  tran‐
       script  of  e2fsck's  output  will  be  useful  to  me.)  If you have a
       writable file system where the transcript can be stored, the  ssccrriipptt(1)
       program is a handy way to save the output of ee22ffsscckk to a file.

       It is also useful to send the output of dduummppee22ffss(8).  If a specific in‐
       ode or inodes seems to be giving ee22ffsscckk trouble, try  running  the  ddee‐‐
       bbuuggffss(8) command and send the output of the ssttaatt(1u) command run on the
       relevant inode(s).  If the inode is a directory, the ddeebbuuggffss _d_u_m_p  com‐
       mand  will  allow  you  to extract the contents of the directory inode,
       which can sent to me after being first run  through  uuuueennccooddee(1).   The
       most useful data you can send to help reproduce the bug is a compressed
       raw image dump of the file system, generated using ee22iimmaaggee(8).  See the
       ee22iimmaaggee(8) man page for more details.

       Always include the full version string which ee22ffsscckk displays when it is
       run, so I know which version you are running.

